WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.220
Liquid nitrogen appears to have flowed across Pluto, captured in fresh images from a spacecraft that left Earth in 2006. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 196. New Horizons data from the 2015 flyby show surface features consistent with past liquid nitrogen flows. The spacecraft captured these observations during its close encounter with the distant world. The findings point to geological activity involving volatile ices.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.740
On-going analysis of archived images may reveal how often such flows occurred.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.700
New horizons' data reveal possible past flows of liquid nitrogen on Pluto, suggesting

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.200
the distant world once experienced enough warmth or internal heat for ISIS to become mobile.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.500
The spacecraft's instruments captured surface features that resemble channels and deposits

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.080
seen on other icy bodies.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:59.060
These observations add to the picture of Pluto as a geologically active object, despite

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.980
it's great distance from the sun. Continued study of the archived images may clarify how often

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.560
such flows occurred and what triggered them. How much of Pluto's surface has been reshaped by

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.740
similar volatile cycles over billions of years. From Pluto's surprising activity, we turn to another

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:24.320
young and active system much closer to home. The planet Elias 2-24b has been confirmed as less than

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.480
1 million years old. It remains the youngest world yet identified by astronomers. The planet

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.260
is still embedded in its natal disk of dusk and gas. Archival data from multiple observatories

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.520
allowed the age determination. Confirmation relied on re-examination of existing telescope records

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:48.160
collected over several years. The object sits within a region where dust and gas continue to

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.820
interact around the host star. No other confirmed planet has an age this low based on current

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.600
observational records. While we study worlds information, agencies on Earth are testing

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.880
new ways to navigate and connect using space-based signals. The European Space Agency carried out

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.260
the first in-orbit demonstration combining connectivity and positioning in a single satellite

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:17.200
system. This marks the first in-orbit test of its kind. The signals combine connectivity

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.280
and positioning services in a single satellite system.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.300
Future satellites could deliver both functions simultaneously to users on the ground.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.860
The demonstration involved signals transmitted from orbit to test dual-purpose performance.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.400
Engineers evaluated how the combined service performed under actual space conditions.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.760
Results from the test will inform designs for later satellite platforms.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.880
The effort was managed through ESA's ongoing technology development programs.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:52.260
As satellite capabilities advance, researchers are also applying new tools to map our nearest neighbor.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:59.540
Researchers are using artificial intelligence to identify previously unseen patterns and clues across the Moon's surface.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.760
The effort processes large volumes of orbital imagery to highlight subtle geological details.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.660
The work aims to support future landing site selection and resource mapping.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:17.480
Artificial intelligence algorithms scan high-resolution images collected by multiple lunar orbiters.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.740
The approach flags surface textures that human review might overlook in initial passes.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.700
Output maps will feed into planning for upcoming robotic and crude missions.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.980
Data handling relies on existing archives rather than new spacecraft observations.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:42.440
While machines help us read planetary surfaces, natural water cycles are quietly shifting something far closer, Earth's own center of mass.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:52.940
Snow, monsoons, and ocean currents redistribute enough water each year to shift Earth's center of mass by fractions of an inch relative to its geometric center.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:04:02.600
satellite tracking has measured these annual oscillations. The center of mass serves as a key reference for satellite navigation and precise elevation measurements.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:09.780
Seasonal redistribution occurs through combined effects of snowfall accumulation and monsoon rainfall patterns.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:15.720
Ocean current variations add further movement on top of the atmospheric and terrestrial contributions.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.060
Measurements come from continuous satellite laser ranging and gravity field monitoring.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.840
monitoring. The shifts remain small yet require accounting in high precision geodetic applications.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.320
Earth's subtle movements remind us that space weather can also leave unexpected marks

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:39.160
on human infrastructure. A geomagnetic storm in 1848 induced currents in telegraph wires

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:45.560
that caused a 16-minute delay on an early British railway line. Researchers traced the incident

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:50.960
through historical records and railway logs. The event offers one of the earliest documented

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:57.080
impacts of space weather on ground infrastructure. Railway operators recorded the exact timing

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:02.280
of the schedule deviation in official logs. The case was reconstructed using preserved

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.060
correspondence between telegraph companies and railway management. No comparable early incidents

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.820
had been linked to geomagnetic activity with this level of detail. Historical surprises give way

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:19.840
to present day institutional shifts. The Texas A and NM University system has received approval

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.280
from its regions to submit a bid for operating NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.000
The laboratory is currently managed by Caltech under a NASA contract.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.000
A successful bid would shift day-to-day management responsibilities.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.320
Regions granted formal authorization for the university's system to prepare and submit

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.080
its proposal.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.780
The Jet Propulsion Laboratory handles multiple NASA planetary science and exploration missions.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:51.240
Management transition would occur only if NASA selects the new operator in the upcoming contract cycle.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.580
Institutional changes often recognize past innovation, as seen in recent ESA awards.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:05.500
The European Space Agency presented awards for excellence and innovation at its industry space days, 2026 event.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.840
The ceremony took place on 16 September at ESA-STECH in the Netherlands.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:15.600
The Awards recognized contributions to space technology and operations.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.200
Three companies received honors during the annual gathering focused on industrial collaboration.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:27.300
The event brought together representatives from ESA programs and European space sector firms.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:33.500
Award categories highlighted specific advances in satellite systems and mission support technologies.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:39.960
Recipients were selected through internal ESA review processes conducted prior to the ceremony.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:45.760
If the sun were reduced to the size of a single grape, sitting on a kitchen counter in Toronto,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.180
the nearest star system would lie roughly 4,000 kilometers away.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.440
That distance matches the span from that counter to a matching grape placed on a counter in Vancouver.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.940
At that scale, the vast emptiness between stars becomes tangible.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.520
You could drive for days across the Canadian prairies and still not reach the next stellar grape.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:12.600
In reality, the average separation between stars in the Milky Way's disc is several light years.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:18.920
A photon leaving our sun would travel for more than four years before arriving at Proxima Centauri,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.240
the closest star.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:26.500
The typical distance to stars visible to the naked eye stretches to dozens or hundreds of light years.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:33.660
This emptiness shapes everything from how planets form to how civilizations might one day travel between systems.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:39.680
Yet, we still do not know whether most stars host planets at all, or whether the gaps themselves

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.980
hide undiscovered worlds too faint or too widely spaced for current telescopes to catch.

